---
title: Proposition 35
---
order: 35
---
short_label: I.35
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Parallelograms which are on the same base and in the same parallels equal one another.
----
proof:

Let {ABCD} and {EBCF} be parallelograms on the same base {BC} and in the same parallels {AF} and {BC}.

I say that {ABCD} equals the parallelogram {EBCF}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="300" height="210" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.35 diagram" src="propI35.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The whole figure stays on screen for every slide; only the angle
    // indicators (angEAB / angFDC) are gated to the slide that references
    // them. Per-slide `highlighted` guides the eye instead of hiding parts.
    var FULL = ["A","B","C","D","E","F","G",
                "BC","AB","CD","BE","CF","DE","AF","AD","EF","AE","DF",
                "ABCD","EBCF","EAB","FDC","DGE","GBC","ABGD","EGCF"];
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "I.35",
        elements: [
            "B;point;free;70,180",
            "C;point;free;160,180",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "A;point;free;30,40",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "D;point;parallelogram;A,B,C",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            // E1 sets how far E' extends past D along the A→D ray (E' = D +
            // dist(A,E1) along that direction). It MUST stay on-canvas: the
            // presentation centres on figureBounds(), which ignores visibility,
            // so a far-off E1/E' would push the whole figure off-screen. At
            // (210,40) it puts E' at the right edge (~x=300); invisible + deferred.
            "E1;point;free;210,40;0;0",
            "E';point;extend;A,D,A,E1;0;0",
            "E;point;lineSegmentSlider;D,E',180,40",
            "BE;line;connect;B,E",
            "F;point;parallelogram;E,B,C",
            "CF;line;connect;C,F",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E",
            "AF;line;connect;A,F",
            "G;point;intersection;CD,BE",
            // Top-parallel sub-segment hover targets (AF is the whole parallel).
            "AD;line;connect;A,D;0;0;0",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F;0;0;0",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E;0;0;0",
            "DF;line;connect;D,F;0;0;0",
            // The two parallelograms as fillable regions (cyan / pink).
            "ABCD;polygon;quadrilateral;A,B,C,D;0;0;0;cyan",
            "EBCF;polygon;quadrilateral;E,B,C,F;0;0;0;pink",
            // Cut-and-paste regions: the congruent triangles (yellow), the
            // subtracted overlap DGE (orange), the added GBC (green), and the
            // two remaining trapezia (cyan). Fill only — the boundary segments
            // are already drawn.
            "EAB;polygon;triangle;E,A,B;0;0;0;yellow",
            "FDC;polygon;triangle;F,D,C;0;0;0;yellow",
            "DGE;polygon;triangle;D,G,E;0;0;0;orange",
            "GBC;polygon;triangle;G,B,C;0;0;0;green",
            "ABGD;polygon;quadrilateral;A,B,G,D;0;0;0;cyan",
            "EGCF;polygon;quadrilateral;E,G,C,F;0;0;0;cyan",
            // Corresponding angles ∠EAB = ∠FDC (magenta). Triangles own EAB/FDC,
            // so the angle markers are angEAB / angFDC.
            "angEAB;sector;angleMarker;A,E,B,22;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angFDC;sector;angleMarker;D,F,C,22;0;0;magenta;magenta"
        ],
        aliases: { "EA": "AE", "FD": "DF", "DC": "CD", "EB": "BE", "FC": "CF" },
        deferDraggables: ["E1"],
        slides: [
            { text: "Let {ABCD} and {EBCF} be parallelograms on the same base {BC} and in the same parallels {AF} and {BC}. I say that {ABCD} equals {EBCF}.",
              visible: FULL,
              highlighted: ["ABCD","EBCF"] },
            { text: "Since {ABCD} is a parallelogram, {AD} equals {BC}. For the same reason {EF} equals {BC}, so {AD} also equals {EF}.",
              visible: FULL,
              highlighted: ["AD","EF","BC"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.34" }] },
            { text: "And {DE} is common, therefore the whole {AE} equals the whole {DF}.",
              visible: FULL,
              highlighted: ["AE","DF","DE"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "C.N.1" }, { ref: "C.N.2" }] },
            { text: "But {AB} also equals {DC}, and the angle {EAB|angEAB} equals the angle {FDC|angFDC}. So the two sides {EA} and {AB} equal {FD} and {DC}, and the triangle {EAB} equals the triangle {FDC}.",
              visible: FULL.concat(["angEAB","angFDC"]),
              highlighted: ["EAB","FDC","angEAB","angFDC"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "angEAB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angFDC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "EAB",    name: geomlib.A.Polygon.superpose, args: { onto: "FDC" } } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.34" }, { ref: "I.29" }, { ref: "I.4" }] },
            { text: "Subtract the triangle {DGE} from each. Then the trapezium {ABGD} which remains equals the trapezium {EGCF} which remains.",
              visible: FULL,
              highlighted: ["ABGD","EGCF"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "ABGD", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "EGCF", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "C.N.3" }] },
            { text: "Add the triangle {GBC} to each. Then the whole parallelogram {ABCD} equals the whole parallelogram {EBCF}.",
              visible: FULL,
              highlighted: ["ABCD","EBCF"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "GBC", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "C.N.2" }] },
            { text: "So parallelograms which are on the same base and in the same parallels equal one another.",
              visible: FULL,
              highlighted: ["ABCD","EBCF"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.4":   "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI4/",
        "I.29":  "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI29/",
        "I.34":  "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI34/",
        "C.N.1": "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn1/",
        "C.N.2": "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn2/",
        "C.N.3": "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn3/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Since {ABCD} is a parallelogram, therefore {AD} equals {BC}. [!just I.34]

For the same reason {EF} equals {BC}, so that {AD} also equals {EF}. And {DE} is common, therefore the whole {AE} equals the whole {DF}. [!just C.N.1; C.N.2]

But {AB} also equals {DC}. Therefore the two sides {EA} and {AB} equal the two sides {FD} and {DC} respectively, and the angle {FDC|angFDC} equals the angle {EAB|angEAB}, the exterior equals the interior. Therefore the base {EB} equals the base {FC}, and the triangle {EAB} equals the triangle {FDC}. [!just I.34; I.29; I.4]

Subtract {DGE} from each. Then the trapezium {ABGD} which remains equals the trapezium {EGCF} which remains. [!just C.N.3]

Add the triangle {GBC} to each. Then the whole parallelogram {ABCD} equals the whole parallelogram {EBCF}. [!just C.N.2]

Therefore *parallelograms which are on the same base and in the same parallels equal one another.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

Euclid's proof specifically treats the case when the point *D* lies between *A* and *E* in which case subtraction of a triangle is necessary. There are other cases to consider, for instance, when *E* lies between *A* and *D.* In that case the point *G* is irrelevant and the trapezium *BCED* may be added to the congruent triangles *ABE* and *DCF* to derive the conclusion. Euclid often supplies a proof for only one case, although occasionally he gives proofs for two or three cases.

